# Next.js + TypeScript Best Practices

- Use file-based routing and keep routes clean and RESTful
- Co-locate components with their usage when appropriate
- Use `srcapp/` directory (Next.js 13+) and server components where possible
- Prefer React Server Components (RSC) for non-interactive content
- Use `use client` directive only when necessary
- Use `async/await` with `fetch()` in server components for data fetching
- Centralize API functions in `services/`
- Use Zod for runtime data validation and TypeScript safety
- Use SWR for client-side data fetching and caching
- Avoid anonymous default exports—use named exports for clarity
- Use Tailwind CSS for scoped styling
- Use `Head` from `next/head` for setting meta tags per page
- Leverage dynamic imports (`next/dynamic`) for heavy components
- Use `getStaticProps`, `getServerSideProps`, or `generateMetadata` for SEO and SSR
- Group constants, types, and interfaces in a `types/` or `constants/` folder
- Prefer composition over inheritance in React components
- Use `eslint`, `prettier`, and `typescript` with strict mode enabled
- Keep components pure and avoid side effects in render
- Use `React.FC<Props>` with clear prop types
- Use `Partial<>`, `Pick<>`, `Omit<>` to shape types
- Avoid prop drilling; use context or Zustand when needed
- Keep `pages` or `routes` directory free of business logic
- Use `.env.local` for environment-specific variables (never commit them)
- Prefer absolute imports (`@/components/...`) for readability
- Keep useEffect logic minimal and dependency arrays accurate
- Use error boundaries and proper error handling (`try/catch`, fallback UIs)
